{
  "id": "maximum-level-sum-of-a-binary-tree",
  "questionId": "maximum-level-sum-of-a-binary-tree",
  "slug": "maximum-level-sum-of-a-binary-tree",
  "title": "Maximum Level Sum of a Binary Tree",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "this is the same level-by-level summing as summing all levels - can you just track the best one as you go, instead of storing every level's sum?",
    "process the queue one level at a time; snapshot its size first so you know exactly how many nodes belong to this level",
    "keep a running (bestSum, bestLevel) pair, updating only with strict '>' so the first (smallest-numbered) level wins any tie",
    "remember levels are 1-indexed, so the very first round of your BFS corresponds to level 1, not level 0"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using '>=' when updating the best level, which lets a later tied level overwrite the correct, smaller-numbered answer",
    "returning a 0-indexed level number instead of the required 1-indexed one",
    "forgetting to initialize the best sum properly, causing an all-negative tree to compare incorrectly against a default of 0"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a\ncomplete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0, at level 1; its children are at level 2, and so on\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node here\"\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]` encodes this tree, where level 3's\nvalues (4+5+6+7=22) sum to more than level 1's (1) or level 2's (2+3=5):\n\n```\nlevel 1:        1\nlevel 2:      2   3\nlevel 3:     4 5 6 7\n```\n\n**Find the level whose node values sum to the largest total, and return that level's number (1-indexed).**\n\nIf two or more levels tie for the largest sum, return the smallest level\nnumber among them.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= length of tree <= 2000",
      "every real node value is in the range -100 to 100",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value",
      "levels are 1-indexed, with the root's level being 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node tree, where the only level is the answer",
      "a tie between two or more level sums, requiring the smallest level number",
      "negative values throughout, where the 'maximum' sum is still negative"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [10, 4, 6]",
        "output": "1",
        "expectedOutput": "1",
        "explanation": "level 1 sums to 10, level 2 sums to 4+6=10 - it's a tie, so the smaller level number, 1, wins"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]",
        "output": "3",
        "expectedOutput": "3",
        "explanation": "level 1 sums to 1, level 2 sums to 5, level 3 sums to 4+5+6+7=22, which is the largest"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [-5]",
        "output": "1",
        "expectedOutput": "1",
        "explanation": "a single node is trivially the level with the maximum (and only) sum"
      }
    ],
